  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 125 PM Tuesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Near-record to record warmth is forecast through \nthe end of the work week as high pressure will dominate the \nsoutheastern United States.\n\nHigh pressure, both at the surface and upper levels, will be the \nprimary feature across the southeastern United States over the next \nfew days. While a backdoor front will approach southern Virginia on \nThursday, it appears that the colder air and precipitation should \nremain to the north of North Carolina. Wednesday is currently \nforecast to be the warmest day. The latest day on the calendar that \nRaleigh has ever reached 90 degrees was on October 10, 1907, but \nthat could be in jeopardy as the current forecast calls for 91 \ndegrees on Wednesday, October 14. As for Greensboro, Wednesday's \nforecast is 87 degrees, and their latest 90 degree day was October \n12, 1904. Finally, our last climate site, Fayetteville, has a \nforecast of 88 degrees, and their latest 90 degree day was October \n26, 1940.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A strong cold front will move across North Carolina \nFriday afternoon/evening, bringing significantly colder weather for \nthe weekend.\n\nLow pressure will develop across the central Plains on Thursday, \nmove over the eastern Great Lakes on Friday, and into eastern Canada \non Saturday. There will not be much moisture locally for the front \nto work with, and the chance of rain will be no higher than 30 \npercent, with most locations remaining dry. With the frontal passage \nstill being 3+ days away, there is some question as to exactly when \nthe front will pass through central North Carolina - an earlier \nfrontal passage would allow more mixing and stronger wind gusts to \noccur, while a later frontal passage would occur during a more \nstable time of day and result in wind gusts not as strong. The \ncurrent forecast calls for the front to move through late Friday \nafternoon, which could bring wind gusts anywhere between 25 and 35 \nmph.\n\nWhether or not there are strong wind gusts Friday afternoon/evening, \nthere will be a stark change in temperatures for the weekend. After \nhighs in the 80s this week, Saturday's and Sunday's highs will only \nbe in the 60s - and a few isolated locations may not rise out of the \n50s despite widespread sunshine. As for low temperatures, Saturday \nnight will be the coldest night, with values ranging from the mid \n30s to the mid 40s. Rural areas outside the Triad will likely be the \ncoldest, and some isolated frost cannot be ruled out. Temperatures \nwill moderate slightly by Monday and Tuesday, with highs in the 70s \nand lows in the 40s.",
